  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1008 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe daylight hours on Wednesday will be dry, but we will be\nwatching our next rain maker taking shape to the northwest. A\nlarge upper low over the Great Lakes will take shape Wednesday\nnight with a shortwave swinging around the southern periphery.\nThis will drag surface low through the Midwest with a cold front\nextending southward. Showers will move into our area ahead of the\nfront after dark with the front clearing the area Thursday \nmorning. That will be the main window for rain chances, but some \nlow PoPs for the northeastern part of the CWA will continue into \nFriday as additional upper level energy pivots around the large \nupper low. The coolest air mass of the season will arrive on \nFriday as well with temperatures struggling to make 50 degrees. \nWidespread 20s look reasonable Saturday morning. There is a high \nchance this will be Nashville's first freeze of the season. For \nreference, the average first freeze date for Nashville is November\n1st. Next weekend looks dry with temperatures slowly warming.",
